  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Above normal temperatures continue this week with\nthe next chance of rainfall coming on Thursday.\n\nOverall, a quiet period is forecast through the remainder of the\nweekend and into early next week as high pressure sets up and\nremains in influence over the Mid-Atlantic and Northeast \nthrough at least Wednesday. While a disorganized shortwave is \nexpected to move through the region late Sunday into Monday, \nmuch of the region will continue to remain dry thanks to the \naforementioned surface high pressure system advecting dry air \ninto the region ahead of this upper level shortwave trough. \nNevertheless, some light showers can't be ruled out in extreme \nSW Virginia and portions of SE West Virginia in upslope \nlocations late tonight into Monday morning. The area looks to \ncompletely dry out once again on Tuesday and most of Wednesday \nacross the region as high pressure slides east off the Mid- \nAtlantic states coastline. \n\nBy early Wednesday, an upper shortwave will eject east from the\nRockies into the Central Plains. Surface low will move into the\nGreat Lakes region by Thursday as a trailing front will push \neast and through the region by early Friday. Guidance has \ncontinued to decrease rainfall amounts over the forecast area \ngiven lesser deep moisture transport from the Gulf and\nAtlantic as a result of the upper trough lifting well north of\nthe region. Regardless, should still see a period of areawide\nrainfall on Thursday, but amounts look to be less than one inch.\n\nPattern throughout the week will continue to support above\nnormal temperatures. Mid 60s to low 70s to start the week, \ntrending to upper 60s to mid 70s by late week.",
